> \emph{Utility ratio} $q=U(\hat b)/\max_b U(b)$ with expected profit $U(b)=p(b)(v-b)$ under held-out probabilities $p$. The calculator returns $\arg\max_b \hat p(b)(v-b)$ and scores $q=0.981$; random choice scores 0.711. \emph{Gap recovered} at budget $c$ is $(q(c)-q(0))/(q_{\text{calc}}-q(0))$; the \emph{dollar gap} uses $U$. Intervals resample the 18 value families within each product (2{,}000 draws); also resampling the three products widens them (Phi-mini: 10--59\%) but leaves every model below the calculator, as does resampling the auctions behind the displayed and scoring curves ($\ge$96\% of 1{,}000 draws). For a second scoring curve (\emph{FP eq.}) we treat each closing price as the second-highest of its $n$ bidders' values, fit a lognormal value distribution $G$ per product by maximum likelihood, and let rivals bid the first-price equilibrium $\beta(x)=x-\int_0^x G^{N-1}/G(x)^{N-1}$ with $N=n+1$, so $b$ wins with probability $G(\beta^{-1}(b))^{n}$ averaged over auctions.
