< The \emph{text reader} does no arithmetic: it splits the trace at each option's mention and takes the last result number written in that option's segment, skipping the inputs. On 150 traces labeled by two LLM annotators (a check, not ground truth), it finds 96\% of stated values and reads 95\% correctly. A second parser marks an option \emph{evaluated} when the trace states its true expected profit within 2\% (92\% precision, 97\% recall per option against the annotators). An option's written value is its true value when the parser finds it and the reader's number otherwise, so a value counts as miscomputed only if the true one appears nowhere. The split in Table~\ref{tab:traces} thus rests mostly on exact matches to computed expected profits (97--100\% of written values for five models, 86\% for Granite-8B). Miscomputed values are 14\% of Granite-8B's written values and at most 3\% for the others, against the annotators' 16\% and $\le$1\%.
