< A text reader that takes the largest value the trace wrote recovers more of the gap than the model's own answer (Table~\ref{tab:main}, Figure~\ref{fig:main}c), but those figures are in-sample, and on the synthetic traces it hurts four models, so it is no replacement for the answer. It works as an audit: sending decisions where reader and answer disagree to the calculator recovers 73--93\% of the gap on products not used to set its rule and 82--100\% on synthetic traces, and it beats random, low-confidence and unfinished-list triggers at the same call rate. With the rule chosen on 17 value families and tested on the 18th it recovers 83--100\%. Why not always call the calculator, or let the model finish? A bidder who builds its own agent should; the audit is for an overseer who sees a third-party agent's reasoning and bid but cannot change its prompt or tools. Incentives matter here: under pay-your-bid the overbids the audit corrects are seller revenue, and the audit lowers it by \$12k--\$78k per 1{,}000 decisions while raising bidder surplus by \$12k--\$35k. Its natural user is therefore the bidder's operator, or a platform whose fee or reputation depends on advertiser value rather than short-run revenue.
